## 2. Background & Motivation

Life insurers must price a policy today for a promise that may not be paid out for decades. Getting this right means combining two sources of uncertainty — whether and when a policyholder dies, and what a future dollar is worth today — into a single premium.

**What I wanted to learn:**
- How do commutation functions (Dx, Nx, Cx, Mx) simplify life-contingent present value calculations?
- How is a net premium derived from the equivalence principle?
- Why does a level-premium policy's reserve rise and then fall back to zero?
- How sensitive is the premium to interest rate, mortality, and policy term?

This project gave me hands-on experience with concepts I'll encounter in Exam FM and Exam FAM.

