The reserve traces an inverted-U shape: it rises for the first 12 years as premium income exceeds the true cost of mortality at younger ages, then falls for the last 8 years as mortality cost overtakes the level premium. The peak reserve is only ~1.1% of face amount, showing how little of a 20-year term premium is really "savings" compared to whole life or an endowment policy.

### 4.2 Sensitivity Analysis

| Scenario | Interest Rate | Mortality Multiplier | Term | Net Annual Premium | % Change |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Base Case | 4% | 1.00x | 20 yrs | $278.92 | 0.0% |
| High Interest (7%) | 7% | 1.00x | 20 yrs | $252.79 | −9.4% |
| Low Interest (2%) | 2% | 1.00x | 20 yrs | $298.55 | 7.0% |
| High Mortality (120%) | 4% | 1.20x | 20 yrs | $334.21 | 19.8% |
| Low Mortality (80%) | 4% | 0.80x | 20 yrs | $223.47 | −19.9% |
| Long Term (30 yrs) | 4% | 1.00x | 30 yrs | $449.85 | 61.3% |
| Short Term (10 yrs) | 4% | 1.00x | 10 yrs | $192.95 | −30.8% |

**Policy term dominates.** Extending the term from 10 to 30 years more than doubles the annual premium, because a longer term pushes the average benefit payment further from issue and exposes the insurer to mortality at much older, higher-qx ages. Mortality assumption is the next largest driver — a 20-percentage-point swing in the multiplier moves the premium by roughly ±20%. Interest rate matters far less than for long-duration liabilities like pensions or whole life, because a 20-year term policy's expected payout is concentrated at older ages where survival probability, not discounting, does most of the work.

### 4.3 Tornado Chart

![Tornado Chart](tornado_chart.png)

Policy term has the widest swing in premium outcomes, followed by mortality assumption, with interest rate producing the narrowest band. This ordering is specific to term insurance and would differ for a product with a longer or investment-linked benefit structure.

