## 6. Limitations & Future Enhancements

**What I simplified:**

*Mortality*
- Used the Ultimate table only (attained-age basis); did not incorporate select-period mortality by policy duration
- No mortality improvement trend applied
- Only one risk class modelled (Male, Non-Smoker, RR100)

*Product & demographic assumptions*
- No lapse or surrender assumptions
- No expense loading — net premiums only, not gross (office) premiums
- Level annual premium only

*Economic assumptions*
- Fixed 4% discount rate for the life of the policy; no stochastic interest rate modelling

*Model structure*
- Single policy, not a portfolio; deterministic reserve calculation only

**Future enhancements:**
- Use the Select & Ultimate table to reflect underwriting selection effects
- Add gross premium calculation with expense and profit loadings
- Model a portfolio of policies across multiple issue ages
- Incorporate lapse rates and compare net vs. modified reserves
- Compare results against published term life insurance quotes to validate pricing

---

## 7. Conclusions

For term life insurance, **policy duration and mortality assumptions matter far more to pricing than interest rate** — the opposite ordering from what dominates long-duration liabilities like pensions. This project reinforced why the same actuarial toolkit (commutation functions, equivalence principle, reserves) produces very different risk profiles across products, and why sensitivity analysis — not just a single base-case number — is essential to understanding a liability.

While simplified for educational purposes, this analysis follows the same fundamental methodology used by professional actuaries pricing and reserving term life insurance products.

